          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The new insight taken as a starting point in
            %em algebraic set theory
            (AST) is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            are in fact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+algebraic+theory"} algebras
            for a suitably presented
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set-theoretic
            conditions (such as well-foundedness) are thereby related to familiar algebraic ones (such as
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+functor"} freeness
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Awodey"} Awodey
          %h2#description_of_the_project Description of the project
          %p
            Also sometimes called
            %em categorical
            (meaning category-theoretic)
            = succeed "," do
              %em set theory
            %em algebraic set theory
            started to be developed in 1988 by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"} André Joyal
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
            and was first presented in detail as a book in 1995 by them. AST is a robust framework based on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            to study and organize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theories
            and to construct
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                models of set theories
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/model+of+a+set+theory"} ?
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            or impredicative;
            %span.newWikiWord
              well founded
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/well-founded+set"} ?
            or ill founded, …), the various constructions of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+hierarchy"} cumulative hierarchy
            of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
            models,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            models and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizability"} realisability
            models.
          %p
            Instead of focusing on categories of sets AST focuses on categories of
            = succeed "es." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+class+structure"} category with class structure
            (a category of classes equipped with a class of small maps (the intuition being that their fibres are small in some sense), powerclasses and a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universal object
            %span.newWikiWord
              ZF-algebras
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Zermelo-Fraenkel+algebra"} ?
            and related structures expressing the idea that the hierarchy of sets is an algebraic structure on the one hand and the interpretation of the first order logic of elementary set theory on the other. The subcategory of sets in a class category is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} elementary topos
            and every elementary topos occurs as the topos of sets in some class category.
          %p
            The class category itself always embeds into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal+completion"} ideal completion
            of a topos. The interpretation of the logic is that in every class category the universe is a model of basic intuitionistic set theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi BIST
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{BIST}
            that is logically
            %span.newWikiWord
              complete
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/complete+model"} ?
            with respect to class category models. Therefore class categories generalize both topos theory and intuitionistic set theory. AST founds and formalizes set theory on the ZF-algebra with operations union and successor (singleton) instead of on the membership relation. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZF axioms
            are nothing but a description of the free ZF-algebra just as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} Peano axioms
            are a description of the free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            on one generator. In this perspective the models of set theory are algebras for a suitably presented
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
          %h2#naming Naming
          %p
            There are two reasons for referring to this research as “algebraic set theory”: The first reason is that the models of set theory that are produced by these methods are algebras for an abstractly presented “theory”, in a precise, technical sense known to category theorists as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            to the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            was the monograph (
            %a{:href => "#JoyalMoerdijk"}> Joyal-Moerdijk 1995
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %em Algebraic set theory
          %p
            The second reason is that we believe the locution “algebraic logic” should properly refer to categorical logic rather than just the logic of Boole and his modern proponents, since categorical logic subsumes such lattice theoretic methods and not the other way around. Hence the term “algebraic set theory” rather than “categorical set theory”. This is in keeping with the use of “algebraic” to mean, essentially, “functorial” in modern algebraic topology, algebraic geometry, etc. (Awodey,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "http://www.phil.cmu.edu/projects/ast/whyast.html"} Why “algebraic set theory”?
          %h3#stack_semantics Stack semantics
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/michaelshulman/show/stack+semantics"} stack semantics
            provides a structural and somewhat more uniform way of treating “classes” in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            theory.
          %h2#see_also See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+classes"} category of classes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+class+structure"} category with class structure
          %h2#references References
          %p Introductions and further pointers are at
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://www.phil.cmu.edu/projects/ast/index.html"} Algebraic Set Theory Website
          %p Two introductions are:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
                ,
                %em An Outline of Algebraic Set Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://web.archive.org/web/20070614063821/https://caae.phil.cmu.edu/projects/ast/Papers/awodey_outline.pdf"}> archived pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benno+van+den+Berg"}> Benno van den Berg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
                %em A Unified Approach to Algebraic Set Theory
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0710.3066"} arXiv
          %p A standard textbook is
          %ul
            %li#JoyalMoerdijk
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic set theory
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 220
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511752483"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511752483
          %p A reference for BIST is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carsten+Butz"} Carsten Butz
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Simpson"} Alex Simpson
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Streicher"} Thomas Streicher
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apal.2013.06.004"} “Relating first-order set theories, toposes and categories of classes”
              %em Annals of Pure and Applied Logic
              165 (2014)
